              "index": 0,
              "id": "call_i3b8AzJvFeQv7C8siJRf6IlY",
              "function": {
                "arguments": "{\"page_summary\":\"This page explains the geological structure of the Alps and summarizes notable high peaks and mountaineering history. It describes the Alps as composed of stacked nappes and layers of rock of European, African and oceanic (Tethyan) origin, with continental European basement rock overlain by marine sediment nappes and nappes derived from the African plate. The Matterhorn is mentioned as an example showing evidence of ongoing orogeny and complex composition. The core Alpine orogenic belt has been folded and fractured, producing steep peaks; examples named include Mont Blanc, the Matterhorn and high peaks in the Pennine Alps and Hohe Tauern. The page also covers the UIAA definition of \\\"four-thousanders\\\" (82 \\\"official\\\" Alpine summits at or above 4,000 m), lists the 22 with at least 500 m prominence, and gives a brief history of ascents: first Mont Blanc ascent in 1786, the Matterhorn in 1865 ending the golden age of alpinism, Karl Blodig's completion of major 4,000 m ascents in 1901, the first British and first female Mont Blanc ascents, Edward Whymper reaching the Matterhorn summit in 1865, and the 1938 first ascent of the Eiger Nordwand.\",\"keyfact\":\"The Union Internationale des Associations d'Alpinisme (UIAA) has defined a list of 82 \\\"official\\\" Alpine summits that reach at least 4,000 m (13,123 ft).\",\"images\":[{\"image_summary\":\"Photograph of alpine mountain peaks with snow-capped summits and a rocky foreground. The image shows glaciated peaks and ridgelines under a clear sky.\",\"keyfact\":\"Caption notes the exposed crystalline basement is made of high-pressure subduction rocks such as blueschist.\"}]}",
                "name": "PageDescription"
              },
              "type": "function"
            }
          ]
        },
        "finish_reason": "tool_calls",
        "index": 0,
